      content:
          '**Differential Diagnosis for Warm, Swollen Extremity with Limited ROM Post-SCI**:\n\n1. **Heterotopic Ossification (HO)** -- Most likely\n - Timing: Typically develops 1-6 months post-SCI (peak at 2-3 months)\n - Presentation: Progressive ROM loss, periarticular warmth/swelling, firm mass, increased spasticity, low-grade fever\n - Location: Hip is the most common site (70-80%), followed by knee, shoulder, elbow\n - Risk factors: Complete SCI, spasticity, pressure ulcers, UTI, DVT -- this patient has several\n\n2. **Deep Vein Thrombosis (DVT)** -- Must rule out\n - Can present with limb swelling, warmth, low-grade fever\n - However, DVT typically causes diffuse limb edema (not a firm periarticular mass) and does NOT cause ROM limitation\n - Patient is on LMWH prophylaxis but DVT can still occur\n\n3. **Fracture** (pathologic/insufficiency)\n - SCI patients develop rapid osteoporosis below the level of injury\n - Can occur with minimal trauma (transfers, ROM exercises)\n - Would present acutely, not progressively over 2 weeks\n - Typically involves long bones, not periarticular regions\n\n4. **Abscess/Cellulitis**\n - Would show erythema, fluctuance, more systemic signs of infection\n - No open wounds or recent injections in the area\n\n5. **Septic Arthritis**\n - Less likely without systemic sepsis, prior joint instrumentation, or bacteremia source\n\n**Diagnostic Workup**:\n- **Triple-Phase Bone Scan**: **Gold standard for early detection**. Phase 1 (flow) and Phase 2 (blood pool) show increased uptake within days of HO onset. Phase 3 (delayed) shows focal periarticular uptake. Positive before X-ray changes are visible.\n- **Alkaline Phosphatase (ALP)**: **Elevated** in active HO (often 2-3x normal). Serial levels track disease activity. Normal ALP does not rule out HO but declining levels suggest maturation.\n- **Plain Radiographs**: May be **normal in early HO** (first 2-4 weeks). Eventually show periarticular calcification/ossification. Useful for monitoring progression.\n- **CT Scan**: Better anatomic detail than X-ray; helpful for surgical planning if excision is considered later\n- **Doppler Ultrasound of Left Lower Extremity**: To **rule out concurrent DVT** (HO and DVT can coexist and HO can compress venous structures causing secondary DVT)\n- **CBC, ESR, CRP**: Inflammatory markers are elevated in HO but help distinguish from infection (very high WBC with left shift suggests infection)\n- **Serum Calcium/Phosphate**: Rule out hypercalcemia of immobilization (which can also occur in this timeframe)',
