      content:
          '**Medical Management**:\n\n**1. NSAID Prophylaxis/Treatment -- Indomethacin**:\n- **Indomethacin 75 mg daily** (extended release) for 6 weeks is the first-line pharmacologic treatment\n- Mechanism: Inhibits prostaglandin-mediated osteoblast differentiation, slowing new bone formation\n- Must use GI prophylaxis: omeprazole 20 mg daily (indomethacin has high GI bleed risk)\n- Monitor renal function (BMP at 2 and 4 weeks)\n- Alternative: Celecoxib 200 mg BID (fewer GI side effects, similar efficacy in some studies)\n- **Contraindications to consider**: Active GI bleed, renal insufficiency, concomitant anticoagulation (weigh DVT prophylaxis overlap carefully)\n\n**2. Etidronate Disodium (Didronel)**:\n- 20 mg/kg/day for 2 weeks, then 10 mg/kg/day for 10 weeks\n- Bisphosphonate that inhibits hydroxyapatite crystal formation\n- Historically used but less favored currently due to osteomalacia risk and limited evidence compared to NSAIDs\n- May be considered if NSAIDs are contraindicated\n\n**3. Range of Motion Exercises**:\n- **Continue gentle passive ROM** within pain-free/resistance-free range\n- **DO NOT perform aggressive or forceful stretching** -- this can worsen HO by causing microtrauma and further inflammation\n- Goal: Maintain current functional ROM; prevent further contracture\n- Low-load, prolonged-duration stretching is preferred\n- Serial goniometry weekly to track ROM changes\n\n**4. Radiation Therapy (for Prophylaxis/Early Treatment)**:\n- **Single fraction radiation 700-800 cGy** to the affected hip\n- Most effective as **prophylaxis** (pre- or post-operatively) or in very early HO\n- Mechanism: Destroys osteoprogenitor cells that are rapidly dividing\n- Can be considered in this case given the active/early nature\n- Requires radiation oncology referral\n- Risk: Long-term malignancy risk (low but non-zero, especially in a 32-year-old)\n\n**5. Spasticity Management**:\n- Optimize baclofen (may need dose increase given worsening spasticity)\n- Spasticity can both contribute to and be exacerbated by HO\n- Consider intrathecal baclofen evaluation if oral medications are insufficient\n\n**6. Surgical Excision -- Timing and Indications**:\n- **NOT indicated now** -- only for mature HO causing significant functional limitation\n- Timing: **12-18 months** after onset, when ALP has normalized and bone scan shows only Phase 3 uptake\n- Indications: Significant functional impairment (inability to sit, transfer, or maintain hygiene), skin breakdown over bony prominence, neurovascular compression\n- Pre-operative radiation (single dose, 24 hours pre-op) + post-operative indomethacin (6 weeks) to prevent recurrence\n- Recurrence rate: 15-25% even with prophylaxis\n\n**7. Monitoring Plan**:\n- Serial ALP every 2-4 weeks (trending down = good response)\n- Repeat plain X-rays every 6-8 weeks to assess progression\n- Clinical ROM measurements weekly\n- Repeat bone scan at 3-6 months to assess maturity\n- Doppler ultrasound if any signs of DVT (HO can compress femoral vein)',
